# Restow web edge: serves the built SPA, terminates TLS (automatic Let's Encrypt for
# RESTOW_APP_DOMAIN) and reverse-proxies the API. better-auth (/api/auth/*) and the REST
# surface (/api/v1/*) both live under /api and go to the api service; everything else is
# the single-page app.

# Where the certificate of the site comes from: RESTOW_EDGE_TLS (.env, default `acme`).
#   acme      Caddy's automatic HTTPS, as for every Caddyfile: Let's Encrypt for a public
#             domain, Caddy's own local authority for localhost, an IP address or an
#             internal name. This is what every installation did before the variable
#             existed, and the snippets below add nothing to the configuration.
#   internal  Caddy's own local certificate authority, whatever the name of the site.
#             For an edge that sits behind another reverse proxy (the installer's
#             --behind-proxy): the proxy reaches it over HTTPS at https://<this host>:443
#             and no public certificate is needed, so no public DNS record and no
#             inbound port 80. A proxy that sends no SNI (nginx does not send one for an
#             address) still gets the certificate of RESTOW_APP_DOMAIN (`default_sni`).
#             The root certificate of that authority is /data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/
#             root.crt in the caddy-data volume; a proxy may verify the edge with it.
# Any other value stops Caddy at start with `File to import not found: restow_tls_<value>`.
# The snippets stand before the global options because those import one of them.
(restow_tls_acme) {
}
(restow_tls_internal) {
	tls internal
}
(restow_sni_acme) {
}
(restow_sni_internal) {
	default_sni {$RESTOW_APP_DOMAIN}
}

{
	servers {
		# Peers whose X-Forwarded-For this Caddy keeps (and appends to) instead of
		# replacing it with the peer's own address. The default trusts loopback only,
		# which no client ever connects from, so nothing changes for a normal
		# installation. Set RESTOW_EDGE_TRUSTED_PROXIES (space-separated CIDR ranges, or
		# `private_ranges`) only when another reverse proxy sits in front of this one,
		# as with the co-hosted public demo (deploy/demo/README.md) or an installation
		# behind a reverse proxy of its own (the installer's --behind-proxy).
		trusted_proxies static {$RESTOW_EDGE_TRUSTED_PROXIES:127.0.0.1/32 ::1/128}
	}
	import restow_sni_{$RESTOW_EDGE_TLS:acme}
}

# Security headers shared by every site this edge serves (the main SPA below and the
# co-hosted demo's own inner Caddy, deploy/caddy-sites/README.md: both bake in this
# same file via the `web` Docker target, so a change here reaches both). Split into a
# plain base (imported both for normal responses and, again, inside handle_errors
# below — a named matcher cannot be defined twice in one site block, so the
# HSTS-gating matcher lives only in the wrapper that normal responses use) and that
# wrapper, `security_headers`.
#
# An unset {$VAR} substituted straight into a header value still puts that header on
# the wire with an empty value (Caddy's `header` directive does not treat "" as
# "omit"), so HSTS below is gated with an `expression` matcher against
# {env.RESTOW_EDGE_HSTS} instead, which omits the header outright when the variable is
# unset. A reverse_proxy failure (the api container unreachable) answers with Caddy's
# own error page before the normal response-header chain runs, which is what
# handle_errors below is for: without it, an outage would otherwise serve a 502 with
# the Server header back and none of the hardening below.
#
# The restore explorer's EML preview and print view are sandboxed `<iframe
# srcdoc="...">` (own `sandbox`, no `allow-scripts`) with their own `<meta
# http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy">` for mail content specifically — but a srcdoc
# iframe has no response of its own to carry an HTTP CSP header, so it still inherits
# this edge-level policy underneath its meta one. That inheritance is why img-src
# allows data: (the preview's embedded `cid:` images, resolved to data URIs) and
# style-src carries 'unsafe-inline' (the mail's own inline styles) below; without
# either, a real inline image or style in a previewed message is silently dropped.
#
# Every header below is set with Caddy's `?` operator (set only if the response does
# not already carry that header), not a bare name. An upstream that sends its own
# hardened headers must keep them — the M365 consent-landing page
# (apps/api/src/features/sources/landing.ts, LANDING_CSP) answers GET
# /api/v1/sources/m365/consent/callback with `Content-Security-Policy: default-src
# 'none'; ...` and `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer` — a bare header name here would
# silently replace both with this edge's own, looser defaults on every response through
# /api/*, undoing hardening the api applied on purpose (consent-redirect.test.ts
# checks default-src 'none' at the api). `?` leaves an already-set header alone and
# only fills the gap on routes that set nothing of their own: the SPA, /assets/*, and
# the rest of /api/*.
#
# Each `?field value` is its own `header` directive rather than several lines inside
# one `header { }` block: Caddy compiles a block's `?` lines into a single handler
# whose "only if unset" condition is one combined requirement that every field in the
# block is absent, not five independent per-field checks (confirmed with `caddy
# adapt`). A route that sets only one of the five, like the consent-landing page above
# (Referrer-Policy and its own CSP, but not X-Frame-Options or Permissions-Policy),
# would then suppress the edge's defaults for all five instead of just the one it set
# itself — silently dropping X-Frame-Options and Permissions-Policy on that route. Five
# separate directives give each header its own independent gate.
(security_headers_base) {
	# Browsers must not guess a response's type from its content.
	header ?X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
	# The destination's origin, never the full URL, leaves Restow for another site.
	header ?Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
	# No site may frame Restow (legacy header; frame-ancestors below is the
	# modern, authoritative version of the same rule).
	header ?X-Frame-Options "DENY"
	# Every browser feature Restow has no use for, off everywhere; anything not
	# listed (including WebAuthn's publickey-credentials-*) keeps its default,
	# which already covers the same-origin passkey flow Restow actually uses.
	header ?Permissions-Policy "camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), midi=(), magnetometer=(), gyroscope=(), accelerometer=()"
	# The built SPA has no inline <script> (apps/web/index.html loads only the
	# hashed module bundle), so script-src stays at 'self'. style-src carries
	# 'unsafe-inline' for three things a stricter policy blocks: Radix UI's
	# inline `style` attributes at runtime, runtime-injected <style> elements
	# (the stats page's chart colour rules, sonner's toast styles), and the
	# srcdoc mail preview's own inline styles (inherited from this policy, see
	# above). A <style> element falls under style-src-elem, which without its
	# own directive falls back to style-src, not style-src-attr, so a separate
	# style-src-attr directive would cover only the Radix case, be redundant
	# with style-src for it, and do nothing for the <style> elements or the
	# mail preview — left out rather than kept as dead weight. img-src allows
	# data: for the mail preview's embedded `cid:` images and any inline
	# icons/badges rendered as data URIs. frame-src stays same-origin only
	# (the mail preview and print view are same-origin srcdoc iframes);
	# frame-ancestors 'none' keeps Restow itself out of anyone else's frame.
	# Nothing here has a documented use for plugins, a retargeted <base>, or a
	# cross-origin form target.
	header ?Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; frame-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'"
	# Caddy's Server header (just "Caddy", no version); not worth advertising on an
	# installation's edge. -Server covers a response this Caddyfile generates itself
	# (the SPA, assets, error pages); reverse_proxy additionally always sets its own
	# Via header on a proxied response regardless of what the upstream sent, so that
	# is removed too — without it, an /api/* response is otherwise still named Caddy
	# via that header. Unconditional deletes, so unlike the five defaults above they
	# stay fine together in one block: there is no "only if unset" requirement to
	# collapse.
	header {
		-Server
		-Via
	}
}

(security_headers) {
	import security_headers_base

	# HSTS only once an operator has confirmed public mode with a real, TLS-terminated
	# domain: RESTOW_EDGE_HSTS=true, documented in .env.example. Never inferred from
	# RESTOW_APP_DOMAIN alone, because this same file also serves the co-hosted demo's
	# inner Caddy, whose RESTOW_APP_DOMAIN is deliberately ":80" with no TLS of its own
	# (deploy/caddy-sites/demo.caddy.example) — and never for a bare local/IP install.
	@hsts_on expression {env.RESTOW_EDGE_HSTS} == "true"
	header @hsts_on Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains"
}

{$RESTOW_APP_DOMAIN} {
	import restow_tls_{$RESTOW_EDGE_TLS:acme}
	encode zstd gzip
	import security_headers

	# Known limitation, not fixable from this site block: in public mode (a real
	# domain, not ":80") Caddy's automatic HTTP->HTTPS redirect on port 80 is
	# generated internally, before any directive here runs, so it still answers
	# with a bare Server: Caddy header. It carries no other content and 301s
	# straight to HTTPS, where every header above applies normally.

	# A reverse_proxy failure (api unreachable) answers before the block above ever
	# runs, so an outage gets the same hardening headers on its error page too
	# (Server still removed, CSP and the rest still set) instead of Caddy's bare
	# default. No HSTS matcher here: it is already covered by import security_headers
	# above for every successful response, and a browser needs to see it only once to
	# start enforcing it, not on every single response.
	#
	# The same block is the maintenance page
	# (https://docs.restowbackup.com/administrators/updates/). While the api is down (it
	# restarts during an update, or it crashed), reverse_proxy answers 502/503/504 and:
	#   - a browser asking for a page gets the static maintenance page in its language
	#     (German when Accept-Language leads with "de", else English; the files are built
	#     with the web app into /srv/maintenance/), with status 503 and Retry-After;
	#   - a request under /api/* gets a small problem+json 503 (the web app treats it as
	#     "the server is restarting", not as an error);
	#   - the read-only status the maintenance page and the web app poll
	#     (/_maintenance/status) answers 503 {"phase":"unavailable"} when no updater runs.
	# Anything else keeps the plain one-line answer.
	handle_errors {
		import security_headers_base

		@maintenance_status path /_maintenance/status
		handle @maintenance_status {
			header Cache-Control "no-store"
			header Content-Type "application/json; charset=utf-8"
			respond `{"phase":"unavailable"}` 503
		}

		# A body over the edge's limit (request_body below) gets the same problem the api
		# answers for one over its own (apps/api/src/middleware/body-limit.ts).
		@body_too_large {
			expression {err.status_code} == 413
			path /api/* /agent/v1/*
		}
		handle @body_too_large {
			header Cache-Control "no-store"
			header Content-Type "application/problem+json; charset=utf-8"
			respond `{"type":"urn:restow:problem:payload-too-large","title":"Payload Too Large","status":413,"detail":"The request body is larger than this endpoint accepts."}` 413
		}

		@app_down_api {
			expression {err.status_code} in [502, 503, 504]
			path /api/*
		}
		handle @app_down_api {
			header Cache-Control "no-store"
			header Retry-After "30"
			header Content-Type "application/problem+json; charset=utf-8"
			respond `{"type":"urn:restow:problem:service-unavailable","title":"Service unavailable","status":503,"detail":"The server is restarting or under maintenance."}` 503
		}

		@app_down_page_de {
			expression {err.status_code} in [502, 503, 504]
			header Accept *text/html*
			header_regexp language Accept-Language (?i)^\s*de
		}
		handle @app_down_page_de {
			header Cache-Control "no-store"
			header Retry-After "30"
			rewrite * /maintenance/index.de.html
			root * /srv
			file_server {
				status 503
			}
		}

		@app_down_page {
			expression {err.status_code} in [502, 503, 504]
			header Accept *text/html*
		}
		handle @app_down_page {
			header Cache-Control "no-store"
			header Retry-After "30"
			rewrite * /maintenance/index.en.html
			root * /srv
			file_server {
				status 503
			}
		}

		handle {
			respond "{err.status_code} {err.status_text}" {err.status_code}
		}
	}

	# Liveness/readiness are the api's own job, not the SPA fallback below.
	# /readyz reports only {status, checks: {database, worker, scheduler}}
	# (apps/api/src/app.ts) — nothing host- or install-specific — so it is safe to
	# expose on the public edge. (Only a caller that presents the opt-in updater's shared
	# secret also gets the running version; nobody else can.)
	handle /healthz {
		reverse_proxy api:3000
	}
	handle /readyz {
		reverse_proxy api:3000
	}

	# Request body limits: the api enforces the exact limit of every route
	# (apps/api/src/middleware/body-limit.ts: 1 MiB unless listed there); the edge sets
	# the outer bound, so an oversized body is cut off before it reaches the api. The
	# routes nobody signs in for (sign-in, the setup wizard, set-password links) get the
	# api's default of 1 MiB right here; everything else under /api is bounded by the
	# largest body the api accepts at all, an import upload segment (32 MiB).
	handle /api/* {
		@api_public path /api/auth/* /api/v1/setup /api/v1/setup/* /api/v1/accounts/*
		request_body @api_public {
			max_size 1MiB
		}
		request_body {
			max_size 33MiB
		}
		reverse_proxy api:3000
	}

	# Endpoint backup (https://docs.restowbackup.com/administrators/endpoint-backup/overview/),
	# all served by the api and none of it behind a browser session:
	#   /agent/v1/*       the agent API (JSON, HTTP Basic with the agent's own secret);
	#                     bodies up to 16 MiB (a run report), enrollment (no secret yet)
	#                     up to 1 MiB, the same limits the api applies
	#   /agent/restic/*   the restic REST backend the agents write their backups through
	#                     (append-only for an agent, streamed in both directions: uploads
	#                     are packs up to 128 MiB, so nothing is buffered here and no body
	#                     size limit is set at the edge; the api refuses more than 128 MiB)
	#   /install/*        the install scripts and the agent binaries with their SHA256SUMS
	# Without these two blocks the single-page app fallback below would answer index.html
	# to an agent, which then fails on the first JSON parse.
	handle /agent/* {
		@agent_enroll path /agent/v1/enroll
		request_body @agent_enroll {
			max_size 1MiB
		}
		@agent_api path /agent/v1/*
		request_body @agent_api {
			max_size 16MiB
		}
		# restow-pve (Proxmox VE): frames of up to 16 VM disk blocks of 4 MiB; the
		# api checks the exact limit of every route. /agent/pve/restic/* streams
		# like /agent/restic/*.
		@pve_api path /agent/pve/v1/*
		request_body @pve_api {
			max_size 80MiB
		}
		reverse_proxy api:3000
	}
	handle /install/* {
		reverse_proxy api:3000
	}

	# The update status while the api may be down: a read-only JSON document the opt-in
	# updater serves (phase, step, progress, message; no version and nothing sensitive,
	# apps/api/src/updater/protocol.ts PublicStatus). Only GET/HEAD, only this one path,
	# no cookie or credentials forwarded. Without an updater the upstream does not
	# resolve and handle_errors above answers.
	handle /_maintenance/status {
		@status_read method GET HEAD
		handle @status_read {
			rewrite * /public/status
			reverse_proxy updater:8090 {
				header_up -Cookie
				header_up -Authorization
				transport http {
					dial_timeout 2s
					response_header_timeout 3s
				}
			}
		}
		respond 405
	}

	# The static maintenance page and its script and styles: needed exactly when the
	# api is down, so they never touch it.
	handle /maintenance/* {
		header Cache-Control "no-cache"
		root * /srv
		file_server
	}

	# The license notices of the web interface, as plain text (the image copies
	# THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md of the release to /srv/licenses/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt;
	# the About box of the settings links to it). Static files that never touch the
	# api, so they open while it is down too; an unknown name is a 404, never the SPA.
	handle /licenses/* {
		header Cache-Control "no-cache"
		@license_text path *.txt
		header @license_text Content-Type "text/plain; charset=utf-8"
		root * /srv
		file_server
	}

	# The content-hashed bundle under /assets/* never changes for a given filename,
	# so it caches for a year; index.html (the catch-all below) must always
	# revalidate, so a redeploy is picked up immediately. /api/* above carries no
	# cache directive of its own here, unchanged either way.
	handle /assets/* {
		root * /srv
		# Only once the file actually exists: without this matcher, a request
		# under /assets/* for a file the current build does not ship (a stale
		# deep link after a redeploy, or a probe) would get the year-long
		# immutable header on its 404 too, which an intermediary cache could
		# then hold onto for a year.
		@asset_file file
		header @asset_file Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
		file_server
	}

	# A page navigation first asks the api whether it is there (a liveness call, nothing
	# is forwarded but the request line): if it is not, the reverse_proxy failure ends in
	# the maintenance page above instead of a shell that cannot load its data.
	@page_navigation {
		method GET
		header Accept *text/html*
	}
	handle @page_navigation {
		route {
			forward_auth api:3000 {
				uri /healthz
				header_up -Cookie
				header_up -Authorization
			}
			header Cache-Control "no-cache"
			root * /srv
			try_files {path} /index.html
			file_server
		}
	}

	handle {
		header Cache-Control "no-cache"
		root * /srv
		try_files {path} /index.html
		file_server
	}
}

# Additional sites served by this same edge, one `*.caddy` file each, from
# deploy/caddy-sites/ (mounted read-only at /etc/caddy/sites by docker-compose.yml).
# Nothing is active by default: the directory ships without any `*.caddy` file, and
# an import glob that matches no file (or a directory that is not mounted at all, as
# in the public demo's own web container) is accepted and imports nothing.
import /etc/caddy/sites/*.caddy
